  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 70-year-old man with hypertension, diabetes, left ventricular dysfunction with LBBB, prior left colectomy for diverticulitis, GERD, PUD, and a history of GI bleeding presents after a recent admission to an outside hospital for recurrent hematochezia and change in stool caliber. CT angiography was normal, his vital signs and hemoglobin remained stable, so he was discharged home without further work-up. What further follow-up, if any, is recommended?",
    "recommendations": "She should be evaluated in the GI clinic within two weeks via an urgent referral for expedited colonoscopy. Order a complete blood count and iron studies in the interim. Fiber supplements are appropriate, and there is no indication for proton pump inhibitor therapy specifically to reduce bleeding risk. Advise the patient to avoid NSAIDs and use acetaminophen for pain. Seek immediate care for large-volume bleeding, dizziness, or syncope. Please initiate a referral order to GI to arrange his assessment and colonoscopic evaluation."
